      "math_academy_id": "q-344266",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-1,1]$, where $f$ is $2$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Because $f$ is $2$-periodic on $[-1,1]$, we have $L=1$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis \n\n$$\ncos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1})\\,\\text{and}\\,sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{1}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is odd, its Fourier series contains only sine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an odd function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a sine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$",
